<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page4-zero-sum" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Two-Player Zero-Sum Games</title>

  <p>
    So far, every time we've looked for an equilibrium, we've asked about best
    responses: what's my best move, given what you're doing? In this section
    we'll look at a different way to think about strategy, which is to play
    it safe. Then we'll see that in a certain kind of game, playing it safe
    and playing a Nash equilibrium turn out to be exactly the same thing!
  </p>

  <p>
    We'll build up the new ideas first, and then you'll read the book's
    version.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Part 1: maximin notation                                     -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Suppose Player 1 commits to a strategy <m>\sigma_1</m>. What's the worst
    that could happen to her? Player 2 could play anything, so we look at
    the smallest payoff Player 1 could get as we range over all of Player 2's
    possible strategies <m>\sigma_2</m>:
    <me>
      m_1(\sigma_1) = \min_{\sigma_2} \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)
    </me>
    So if Player 1 plays <m>\sigma_1</m>, then <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m> is the
    smallest possible payoff she can get. It's her worst case.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's try it on a small example. Here are Player 1's payoffs when she
    plays a strategy called Top, against each of Player 2's three pure
    strategies. (Only Player 1's payoffs are shown.)
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-maximin-top-row">
    <title>Player 1's payoffs when she plays Top</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell>Player 2 plays L</cell>
        <cell>Player 2 plays M</cell>
        <cell>Player 2 plays R</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Player 1 plays Top</cell>
        <cell><m>3</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <exercise xml:id="check-maximin-top-worst-case">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is <m>m_1(\text{Top})</m>?
        <fillin name="mtop" answer="-1" width="3"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate name="mtop">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The worst thing Player 2 can do to her is play M. What
              about mixed strategies for Player 2, since <m>\sigma_2</m>
              ranges over all of them? A mixed strategy gives Player 1 a
              weighted average of <m>3</m>, <m>-1</m>, and <m>2</m>, and an
              average can't be lower than the smallest number being averaged.
              So the worst case is always at one of Player 2's pure
              strategies. Keep this in mind, because it comes up again soon.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="3"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the <em>best</em> case for Top. We want the worst case,
              the smallest payoff Player 2 could leave her with.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="1.3333" tolerance="0.01"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the average of the three payoffs, which is what Player 1
              would get if Player 2 picked each column equally often. But
              <m>m_1</m> asks about the worst Player 2 could do to her, not a
              typical outcome.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000" max="-1.001"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Can Player 2 push her below <m>-1</m>? Even with a mixed
              strategy, Player 1 gets a weighted average of <m>3</m>,
              <m>-1</m>, and <m>2</m>, and an average can't be lower than the
              smallest number being averaged.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Look for the smallest payoff in the row. That's the worst thing
            Player 2 can do to her.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now, Player 1 gets to choose <m>\sigma_1</m>. If she's playing it safe,
    she wants the strategy whose worst case is as good as possible. That's
    called a <term>maximin strategy</term> for Player 1, and the book writes
    it as <m>\sigma_1^\dagger</m>. Her worst case when she plays it is called
    her <term>maximin payoff</term>, written <m>m_1^\dagger</m>:
    <me>
      m_1^\dagger = m_1(\sigma_1^\dagger) = \max_{\sigma_1} m_1(\sigma_1)
      = \max_{\sigma_1} \min_{\sigma_2} \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)
    </me>
    By playing <m>\sigma_1^\dagger</m>, Player 1 guarantees herself at least
    <m>m_1^\dagger</m> no matter what Player 2 does, and no other strategy
    guarantees her more.
  </p>

  <p>
    Careful with the name: it's <em>maximin</em>, the max of the mins.
    <q>Minimax</q> sounds very similar, and it rolls off the tongue a bit
    easier, but it's a different word. The order matters: first take the
    min (the worst case for each strategy), then the max (the best of those
    worst cases).
  </p>

  <p>
    We can define the same things for Player 2: her worst case
    <m>m_2(\sigma_2)</m> for each strategy <m>\sigma_2</m>, her maximin
    strategy <m>\sigma_2^\dagger</m>, and her maximin payoff
    <m>m_2^\dagger</m>. Before you look at the book's version, see if you can
    figure out what the formula should be.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-maximin-player2-formula">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Build the formula for Player 2's maximin payoff
        <m>m_2^\dagger</m> by putting the blocks in order. Not every block
        belongs!
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks layout="horizontal">
      <block><m>m_2^\dagger =</m></block>
      <block><m>\max_{\sigma_2}</m></block>
      <block><m>\min_{\sigma_1}</m></block>
      <block><m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\max_{\sigma_1}</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\min_{\sigma_2}</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m></block>
    </blocks>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Three questions: Whose payoff does Player 2 care about? Which
        strategy does Player 2 get to choose (that's the max)? And whose
        choices make up her worst case (that's the min)?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle m_2^\dagger = \max_{\sigma_2} \min_{\sigma_1}
        \pi_2(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>. For each of her strategies
        <m>\sigma_2</m>, Player 2 finds her worst case over everything
        Player 1 could do. Then she picks the <m>\sigma_2</m> with the best
        worst case. The common mistakes are using <m>\pi_1</m> (Player 2
        cares about <em>her</em> payoff) and swapping which player the max
        and min range over.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Finally, a strategy profile <m>(\sigma_1^\dagger, \sigma_2^\dagger)</m>
    in which <em>both</em> players use maximin strategies is called a
    <term>maximin equilibrium</term>. In a maximin equilibrium, each player
    gets at least her maximin payoff <m>m_i^\dagger</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Part 2: Battle of the Sexes walkthrough                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Let's find the maximin equilibrium for a game we already know, the
    Battle of the Sexes from Section 3.2.4. Alice and Bob each choose
    between a concert and a wrestling match. Here's the payoff matrix with
    their probabilities in the margins.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-bos-pq">
    <title>Battle of the Sexes, with mixed strategies</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="2">Bob</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>q</m></cell>
        <cell><m>{1-q}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell>concert</cell>
        <cell>wrestling</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Alice</cell>
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell>concert</cell>
        <cell>(2, 1)</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>{1-p}</m></cell>
        <cell>wrestling</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(1, 2)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    So Alice's mixed strategy is
    <m>\sigma_1 = p \cdot \text{concert} + (1-p) \cdot \text{wrestling}</m>,
    and Bob's is
    <m>\sigma_2 = q \cdot \text{concert} + (1-q) \cdot \text{wrestling}</m>.
    Like the book, we'll write a strategy profile as <m>(p, q)</m> instead of
    <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>. Alice's expected payoff is
    <me>
      \pi_1(p, q) = 2pq + 1 \cdot (1-p)(1-q)
    </me>
    which we can rearrange as
    <me>
      \pi_1(p, q) = (1 - p) + (3p - 1)q.
    </me>
    Written this way, you can see that if we fix <m>p</m>, then
    <m>\pi_1</m> is a <em>line</em> in <m>q</m> with slope <m>3p - 1</m>. That
    slope is exactly the partial derivative the book computes:
    <me>
      \frac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial q} = 3p - 1, \quad\text{which is }
      \begin{cases}
        \text{negative} \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}, \\
        0 \amp \text{if } p = \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \text{positive} \amp \text{if } \frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1.
      \end{cases}
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    In this notation, Alice's worst case when she plays <m>p</m> is
    <me>
      m_1(p) = \min_{0 \leq q \leq 1} \pi_1(p, q).
    </me>
    To find it, we need to know where the line <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> is lowest
    as <m>q</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m>. Here are the graphs for three
    sample values of <m>p</m>.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bos-pi1-three-p">
    <caption>
      Alice's payoff <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> as a function of Bob's <m>q</m>, for
      <m>p = 0.2</m>, <m>p = \frac{1}{3}</m>, and <m>p = 0.75</m>. The dot
      marks the lowest point on each graph.
    </caption>
    <image width="100%">
      <shortdescription>
        Three graphs of Alice's payoff against Bob's probability q: decreasing
        for p equals 0.2, flat for p equals one third, and increasing for p
        equals 0.75.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          Three side-by-side graphs. Each has Bob's probability q of going to
          the concert on the horizontal axis, from 0 to 1, and Alice's
          expected payoff on the vertical axis, from 0 to 1.6.
        </p>
        <p>
          Left graph, p equals 0.2: a straight line decreasing from 0.8 at q
          equals 0 down to 0.4 at q equals 1. The lowest point, 0.4 at q
          equals 1, is marked with a dot.
        </p>
        <p>
          Middle graph, p equals one third: a horizontal line at height two
          thirds. Every value of q gives the same payoff, two thirds.
        </p>
        <p>
          Right graph, p equals 0.75: a straight line increasing from 0.25 at
          q equals 0 up to 1.5 at q equals 1. The lowest point, 0.25 at q
          equals 0, is marked with a dot.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\footnotesize]
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelA,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$}, ylabel={$\pi_1(p,q)$},
          title={$p = 0.2$},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, very thick] {0.8 - 0.4*x};
          \addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2pt] coordinates {(1,0.4)};
          \node[anchor=south east, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
            at (axis cs:1,0.62) {lowest: $0.4$\\at $q = 1$};
        \end{axis}
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelB,
          at={(panelA.south east)}, anchor=south west, xshift=1.1cm,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$},
          title={$p = \frac{1}{3}$},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, very thick] {2/3};
          \node[anchor=north, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
            at (axis cs:0.5,0.55) {every $q$ gives $\frac{2}{3}$};
        \end{axis}
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelC,
          at={(panelB.south east)}, anchor=south west, xshift=1.1cm,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$},
          title={$p = 0.75$},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, very thick] {0.25 + 1.25*x};
          \addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2pt] coordinates {(0,0.25)};
          \node[anchor=west, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
            at (axis cs:0.3,0.25) {lowest: $0.25$\\at $q = 0$};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-bos-worst-case-fillin">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the graphs to find Alice's worst case for two of the sample
        values of <m>p</m>. Enter the value of <m>q</m> that gives her the
        lowest payoff, and the value of <m>m_1(p)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.2</m>: worst case at <m>q =</m>
        <fillin name="q02" answer="1" width="3"/>,
        so <m>m_1(0.2) =</m>
        <fillin name="m02" answer="0.4" width="3"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.75</m>: worst case at <m>q =</m>
        <fillin name="q075" answer="0" width="3"/>,
        so <m>m_1(0.75) =</m>
        <fillin name="m075" answer="0.25" width="3"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate name="q02">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="0"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              At <m>q = 0</m> the line is at its <em>highest</em>, Alice's
              best case. The line is decreasing, so where is it lowest?
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The line for <m>p = 0.2</m> is decreasing, so it's lowest at one
            of the ends.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="m02">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="0.8" tolerance="0.001"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the highest point on the graph, Alice's best case. We
              want the lowest.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Read the height of the dot on the <m>p = 0.2</m> graph, or plug
            your <m>q</m> into <m>\pi_1(0.2, q) = 0.8 - 0.4q</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="q075">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="1"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              At <m>q = 1</m> the line is at its <em>highest</em>. This time
              the line is increasing, so where is it lowest?
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The line for <m>p = 0.75</m> is increasing, so it's lowest at one
            of the ends.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="m075">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="1.5" tolerance="0.001"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the highest point on the graph, Alice's best case. We
              want the lowest.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Read the height of the dot on the <m>p = 0.75</m> graph, or plug
            your <m>q</m> into <m>\pi_1(0.75, q) = 0.25 + 1.25q</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.2</m>, the line is decreasing, so it's lowest at
        <m>q = 1</m>, and <m>m_1(0.2) = 0.4</m>. Alice's worst case is Bob
        always going to the concert: she's usually at the wrestling match, so
        the worst thing for her is Bob being somewhere else.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.75</m>, the line is increasing, so it's lowest at
        <m>q = 0</m>, and <m>m_1(0.75) = 0.25</m>. Now she's usually at the
        concert, so her worst case is Bob always going to the wrestling
        match.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    And for <m>p = \frac{1}{3}</m>, the line is flat: every <m>q</m> gives
    Alice the same payoff, so any <m>q</m> realizes her worst case, and
    <m>m_1(\frac{1}{3}) = \frac{2}{3}</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same reasoning works for <em>every</em> <m>p</m>, not just these
    three. If <m>0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}</m>, the slope <m>3p - 1</m> is
    negative, so the line is lowest at <m>q = 1</m>. If
    <m>\frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1</m>, the slope is positive, so the line is
    lowest at <m>q = 0</m>. Putting the three cases together:
    <me>
      m_1(p) = \min_{0 \leq q \leq 1} \pi_1(p, q) =
      \begin{cases}
        \pi_1(p, 1) \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \text{any } \pi_1(p, q) \amp \text{if } p = \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \pi_1(p, 0) \amp \text{if } \frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1.
      \end{cases}
    </me>
    Now plug in. Using <m>\pi_1(p, q) = (1 - p) + (3p - 1)q</m>, we get
    <m>\pi_1(p, 1) = 2p</m> and <m>\pi_1(p, 0) = 1 - p</m>, and every
    <m>\pi_1(\frac{1}{3}, q)</m> equals <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>. So
    <me>
      m_1(p) =
      \begin{cases}
        2p \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \frac{2}{3} \amp \text{if } p = \frac{1}{3}, \\
        1 - p \amp \text{if } \frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1.
      \end{cases}
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Here's the graph of <m>m_1(p)</m>. Notice it's always the <em>lower</em>
    of the two lines <m>2p</m> and <m>1 - p</m>, which makes sense: Bob's
    worst-case choice for Alice is always <m>q = 1</m> or <m>q = 0</m>, just
    like in the Top example earlier.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bos-m1-graph">
    <caption>
      Alice's worst case <m>m_1(p)</m> (solid), the lower of the lines
      <m>2p</m> and <m>1 - p</m>. The dashed segments show where each line
      continues above the other.
    </caption>
    <image width="70%">
      <shortdescription>
        Graph of Alice's worst case m1 of p: rising along 2p up to a peak of
        two thirds at p equals one third, then falling along 1 minus p.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A graph with Alice's probability p of going to the concert on the
          horizontal axis, from 0 to 1, and her worst-case payoff on the
          vertical axis, from 0 to 1.2. The horizontal axis is marked at 0,
          one third, and 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          The solid graph of m1 of p is shaped like an upside-down V. From p
          equals 0 to p equals one third, it follows the line 2p, rising from
          0 to two thirds. From p equals one third to p equals 1, it follows
          the line 1 minus p, falling from two thirds to 0. The peak, at the
          point one third, two thirds, is marked with a dot.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dashed segments show the line 2p continuing upward past the peak
          and the line 1 minus p continuing upward to the left of the peak.
          In both cases the dashed part is above the solid graph, showing
          that m1 of p is always the lower of the two lines.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small]
        \begin{axis}[
          width=10cm, height=7cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.08, ymin=0, ymax=1.25,
          xtick={0,0.3333,1}, xticklabels={$0$,$\frac{1}{3}$,$1$},
          ytick={0,0.6667,1}, yticklabels={$0$,$\frac{2}{3}$,$1$},
          xlabel={$p$}, ylabel={$m_1(p)$},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0.3333:0.58, dashed, thick, gray] {2*x};
          \addplot[domain=0:0.3333, dashed, thick, gray] {1 - x};
          \addplot[domain=0:0.3333, very thick] {2*x};
          \addplot[domain=0.3333:1, very thick] {1 - x};
          \addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.6pt] coordinates {(0.3333,0.6667)};
          \node[anchor=west, font=\footnotesize] (peaklabel) at (axis cs:0.62,0.86)
            {peak $\left(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3}\right)$};
          \draw[-&gt;, thin] (peaklabel.west) -- (axis cs:0.355,0.685);
          \node[anchor=north west, font=\footnotesize] at (axis cs:0.13,0.25)
            {$m_1(p) = 2p$};
          \node[anchor=south west, font=\footnotesize] at (axis cs:0.68,0.33)
            {$m_1(p) = 1 - p$};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Remember, a maximin strategy is the one whose worst case is as good as
    possible. In other words, Alice's maximin strategy <m>p^\dagger</m> is
    the value of <m>p</m> that makes <m>m_1(p)</m> as large as possible.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-bos-maximin-p">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the graph, what is Alice's maximin strategy <m>p^\dagger</m>,
        and what is her maximin payoff <m>m_1^\dagger</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = 1</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = 2</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The concert is Alice's favorite, and <m>2</m> is her best
            possible payoff. But if she always goes to the concert, Bob could
            go to the wrestling match, and she'd get <m>0</m>. In fact
            <m>m_1(1) = 0</m>, the lowest point on the graph!
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = \frac{1}{3}</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The highest point of <m>m_1(p)</m> is the peak at
            <m>(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3})</m>. By going to the concert with
            probability <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>, Alice guarantees herself an
            expected payoff of at least <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>, whatever Bob does.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = \frac{1}{3}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Read the peak off the graph again: the first coordinate is
            <m>p^\dagger</m> and the second is <m>m_1^\dagger</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = 0</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = 1</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            If Alice always goes to the wrestling match, Bob could go to the
            concert, and she'd get <m>0</m>. So <m>m_1(0) = 0</m>, not
            <m>1</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    A similar calculation for Bob shows that his maximin strategy is
    <m>q^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m>, and his maximin payoff is
    <m>m_2^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m>. So
    <m>(p^\dagger, q^\dagger) = (\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3})</m> is a maximin
    equilibrium.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="reflect-maximin-in-words">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now that you've worked through all the details, describe in your own
        words what a maximin strategy is. Use Alice as an example: what is
        <m>p^\dagger = \frac{1}{3}</m> doing for her? Something to think
        about: Alice prefers the concert, but her maximin strategy has her go
        to the concert only <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> of the time. Why?
      </p>
      <p>
        Write down your answer (or at least think it through) before you
        reveal ours.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        A maximin strategy is the safest choice: the strategy whose worst
        case is as good as possible. It guarantees you at least your maximin
        payoff, no matter what the other player does.
      </p>
      <p>
        For Alice, <m>p^\dagger = \frac{1}{3}</m> guarantees her an
        expected payoff of at least <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>. Her two possible
        worst cases are Bob always at the concert (she gets <m>2p</m>) and
        Bob always at the wrestling match (she gets <m>1 - p</m>). Playing it
        safe means making the worse of those two as good as possible, which
        happens when they're equal: <m>2p = 1 - p</m>, so
        <m>p = \frac{1}{3}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Why so little concert? Meeting at the concert is worth <m>2</m> to
        her, but meeting at the wrestling match is only worth <m>1</m>. So
        she doesn't need to be at the concert very often to protect herself
        in the <q>Bob at the concert</q> case, but she needs to be at the
        wrestling match a lot to protect herself in the <q>Bob at wrestling</q>
        case. Playing it safe has nothing to do with what she prefers. It's
        all about protecting against the worst outcome.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Here's the punchline: the maximin equilibrium
    <m>(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3})</m> is <em>not</em> a Nash equilibrium.
    For example, if Bob plays <m>q = \frac{2}{3}</m>, then
    <m>\pi_1(p, \frac{2}{3}) = \frac{1}{3} + p</m>, which is increasing in
    <m>p</m>. So Alice's best response is <m>p = 1</m> (always the concert),
    not <m>p = \frac{1}{3}</m>. The Nash equilibria of the Battle of the
    Sexes are <m>(1, 1)</m> (both at the concert), <m>(0, 0)</m> (both at the
    wrestling match), and the mixed one <m>(\frac{2}{3}, \frac{1}{3})</m>.
    So in general, playing it safe and playing a Nash equilibrium are two
    different things.
  </p>

  <p>
    But there's a kind of game where they line up exactly. A two-player game
    is <term>zero-sum</term> if the two players' payoffs add up to <m>0</m>
    in every cell of the payoff matrix: whatever one player wins, the other
    loses. One-Card Two-Round Poker from the last section is an example.
    The Battle of the Sexes is not (look at the cell <m>(2, 1)</m>).
  </p>

  <p>
    The definition only talks about the cells, which come from pure
    strategies. But if the payoffs add to <m>0</m> in every cell, then they
    also add to <m>0</m> for any pair of <em>mixed</em> strategies. Think
    about why!
  </p>

  <p>
    In a zero-sum game, it turns out that maximin equilibria and Nash
    equilibria are exactly the same thing. That's the main theorem of this
    section.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-5-through-theorem">
    <title>Section 5.5, Through Theorem 5.6</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.5 from the beginning through the statement of
      Theorem 5.6.</alert> Stop right before the proof.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything before the theorem is what we just worked through, so you
      can read that part quickly. You'll see the book's notation for
      everything we did: <m>\sigma_1^\dagger</m>, <m>m_1^\dagger</m>, and the
      Battle of the Sexes written in terms of <m>(p, q)</m>. Read the
      statement of Theorem 5.6 carefully, and then we'll break down why it's
      true.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Part 3: why Nash implies maximin                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Let's see why the theorem is true, at least the main part of it. Suppose
    <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium of a two-player
    zero-sum game. We'll show that Player 1's payoff there is her maximin
    payoff:
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1^\dagger.
    </me>
    We'll do this by showing <m>\geq</m> and then <m>\leq</m>.
  </p>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Step 1: <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq m_1^\dagger</m></title>
    <p>
      Take any strategy <m>\sigma_1</m> for Player 1. Then
      <me>
        \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2^*)
        \geq m_1(\sigma_1).
      </me>
      The first inequality is because <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is a
      Nash equilibrium, so Player 1 can't do better by switching to
      <m>\sigma_1</m>. The second is because <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m> is the
      <em>smallest</em> payoff Player 1 can get from <m>\sigma_1</m>, so it's
      no bigger than her payoff against <m>\sigma_2^*</m> in particular.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since <m>\sigma_1</m> was arbitrary, <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>
      is at least as big as every <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m>. So it's at least as
      big as the largest one:
      <me>
        \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq \max_{\sigma_1} m_1(\sigma_1)
        = m_1^\dagger.
      </me>
      Notice we haven't used the fact that the game is zero-sum yet!
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Step 2: <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq m_1^\dagger</m></title>
    <p>
      This is the step where zero-sum matters. First, we'll compare
      <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> to what Player 1 gets when she
      keeps playing <m>\sigma_1^*</m> but Player 2 switches to some other
      strategy <m>\sigma_2</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="check-proof-zero-sum-flip">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that for some strategy <m>\sigma_2</m>,
        <me>
          \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \gt \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2).
        </me>
        Since the game is zero-sum, what else must be true?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \gt \pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Zero-sum means <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m>. What happens to an
            inequality when you multiply both sides by <m>-1</m>?
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \lt \pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Since <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m>, multiplying both sides by
            <m>-1</m> flips the inequality. In words: if Player 1 does
            <em>worse</em> when Player 2 switches to <m>\sigma_2</m>, then
            Player 2 does <em>better</em>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2) \lt 0</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Zero-sum doesn't say anything about whether a single payoff is
            positive or negative. It says the two players' payoffs add to
            <m>0</m>, so <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m>. Use that to compare Player
            2's payoffs.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    But <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \lt \pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)</m>
    says Player 2 could do better by switching to <m>\sigma_2</m>. That's a
    direct contradiction of <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> being a Nash
    equilibrium! So there's no such <m>\sigma_2</m>, which means
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)
      \quad\text{for every } \sigma_2.
    </me>
    This is really the heart of the theorem. In a zero-sum game, Player 2's
    best response is the same as the move that hurts Player 1 the most,
    because every bit Player 2 gains comes out of Player 1's payoff.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-proof-conclude-m1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        We now know <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq
        \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)</m> for every <m>\sigma_2</m>. What can we
        conclude?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1^\dagger</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's where we're headed, but we're not there yet. This
            inequality only compares payoffs where Player 1 plays
            <m>\sigma_1^*</m>. So it tells us about the worst case for
            <m>\sigma_1^*</m> specifically, <m>m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m>, not the
            best worst case over all strategies, <m>m_1^\dagger</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m>, but not necessarily equal.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is true, but we can say more. Is
            <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> itself one of the payoffs
            Player 1 can get when she plays <m>\sigma_1^*</m>?
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Among all the payoffs Player 1 can get by playing
            <m>\sigma_1^*</m>, <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is less
            than or equal to every one of them, <em>and</em> it's one of them
            (the one where Player 2 plays <m>\sigma_2^*</m>). So it's the
            smallest one, which is exactly <m>m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m>. In other
            words, <m>\sigma_2^*</m> is Player 1's worst case.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    To finish Step 2: <m>m_1^\dagger</m> is the largest of all the
    <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m>, so in particular
    <m>m_1(\sigma_1^*) \leq m_1^\dagger</m>. Therefore
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1(\sigma_1^*) \leq m_1^\dagger.
    </me>
  </p>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Putting it together</title>
    <p>
      Steps 1 and 2 together give
      <me>
        \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1(\sigma_1^*) = m_1^\dagger.
      </me>
      The same argument works for Player 2, so
      <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_2(\sigma_2^*) = m_2^\dagger</m>.
      Here's what that tells us:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Since <m>m_1(\sigma_1^*) = m_1^\dagger</m>, the strategy
            <m>\sigma_1^*</m> achieves the best possible worst case, so it's a
            maximin strategy. Same for <m>\sigma_2^*</m>. So every Nash
            equilibrium is a maximin equilibrium. That's half of part (1) of
            the theorem.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            At the Nash equilibrium, each player gets her maximin payoff.
            That's part (2).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Both maximin payoffs come from the same strategy profile
            <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>, and the payoffs there add to
            <m>0</m> because the game is zero-sum. So
            <m>m_1^\dagger + m_2^\dagger = 0</m>. That's part (3). (This
            uses the fact that a Nash equilibrium exists in the first place,
            which Nash's Existence Theorem, Theorem 5.1, guarantees.)
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      The other direction of part (1), that every maximin equilibrium is a
      Nash equilibrium, is also true. The book proves it using part (3), if
      you're curious.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-5-rest">
    <title>Section 5.5, The Rest</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Finish reading Section 5.5.</alert> You can skip or skim the
      book's proof, since we just did the main part of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The part to read carefully starts at the paragraph beginning
      <q>Theorem 5.6 implies that all Nash equilibria...</q> It has a really
      useful consequence of the theorem, and then uses it to finish off
      One-Card Two-Round Poker. We'll go through the poker argument together
      afterward, since the book's version is a bit hard to follow.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Part 4: interchangeability and poker                         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    The first paragraph you just read is the key idea, so let's make sure it
    sticks.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-interchangeability">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m> and <m>(\tau_1, \tau_2)</m> are
        both Nash equilibria of a two-player zero-sum game. Is
        <m>(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m> also a Nash equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. By Theorem 5.6, <m>\sigma_1</m> and <m>\tau_2</m> are both
            maximin strategies, so <m>(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m> is a maximin
            equilibrium, and therefore a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! You can mix and match halves of Nash equilibria in a
            zero-sum game. By part (2), all of them give the same payoffs,
            too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. In any game, you can swap halves of two Nash equilibria and
            get another one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not in every game! In the Battle of the Sexes, (concert, concert)
            and (wrestling, wrestling) are both Nash equilibria, but if you
            swap halves you get (concert, wrestling). Alice and Bob miss each
            other and both get <m>0</m>, so it's not a Nash equilibrium. This
            only works because the game is zero-sum.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Only if <m>\sigma_1 = \tau_1</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's too strict. Theorem 5.6 says <em>any</em> maximin strategy
            for Player 1 can be paired with <em>any</em> maximin strategy for
            Player 2.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            There's no way to tell without seeing the payoffs.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Theorem 5.6 answers this for every two-player zero-sum game, no
            matter what the payoffs are. Which part of the theorem connects
            Nash equilibria and maximin strategies?
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's see what this buys us for poker. In Section 5.4 we found a
    Nash equilibrium where both players use all three of their strategies:
    <me>
      \left(\tfrac{1}{5}\,bb + \tfrac{2}{5}\,bf + \tfrac{2}{5}\,f, \quad
      \tfrac{8}{15}\,bb + \tfrac{2}{15}\,bf + \tfrac{1}{3}\,f\right).
    </me>
    The end of Section 5.4 also points out something the calculation
    showed along the way. When we solved Player 2's indifference equations
    for <m>p_1, p_2, p_3</m>, we only used two things: Player 2's three
    strategies earn him the same payoff, and the <m>p_i</m> add up to
    <m>1</m>. We never assumed anything about which of Player 1's strategies
    were active. And those equations have only one solution. So:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <alert>Fact from 5.4:</alert> In <em>any</em> Nash equilibrium where
      Player 2 uses all three of his strategies, Player 1 must play
      <m>\frac{1}{5}\,bb + \frac{2}{5}\,bf + \frac{2}{5}\,f</m>. (And the same
      the other way around: if Player 1 uses all three, Player 2 must play
      <m>\frac{8}{15}\,bb + \frac{2}{15}\,bf + \frac{1}{3}\,f</m>.)
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Now take <em>any</em> Nash equilibrium <m>(\tau_1, \tau_2)</m> of the
    poker game.
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          By interchangeability, we can swap in Player 2's strategy from the
          equilibrium we found. So
          <m>\left(\tau_1,\ \frac{8}{15}\,bb + \frac{2}{15}\,bf +
          \frac{1}{3}\,f\right)</m> is also a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          In that Nash equilibrium, Player 2 uses all three of his
          strategies.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          So by the fact from 5.4,
          <m>\tau_1 = \frac{1}{5}\,bb + \frac{2}{5}\,bf + \frac{2}{5}\,f</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    The same argument with the players switched shows
    <m>\tau_2 = \frac{8}{15}\,bb + \frac{2}{15}\,bf + \frac{1}{3}\,f</m>. So
    <m>(\tau_1, \tau_2)</m> is the equilibrium we already found. It's the
    only Nash equilibrium of the game!
  </p>

  <p>
    The book phrases this as a proof by contradiction (suppose there were an
    equilibrium where each player used only one or two strategies, then
    swap halves...), but it's the same idea. This also settles the cases
    with two active strategies that the book skipped back in Section 5.4:
    there are no Nash equilibria of those types.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-5" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.5" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
